   "text": "The pattern is a single primitive pseudo-cubic perovskite, and its intensities match the sodium tungsten bronze (mp-1221403).\n\n- **Peak positions:** Peaks fall at about 23.0, 32.5, 40, 46.4, 52.2, 57.6, 67.6, 72.4 and 77 \u00b02\u03b8. These index as (100), (110), (111), (200), (210), (211), (220), (300) and (310) of a primitive cubic cell with a \u2248 3.86\u20133.89 \u00c5.\n- **Candidate cells:** Na(WO3)3 (mp-1221403) has a hexagonal cell (a = 5.507, c = 6.743 \u00c5) equivalent to a pseudo-cubic perovskite with a \u2248 3.89 \u00c5. SrCoO3 (mp-1283849, a \u2248 3.90 \u00c5) has a similar cell. Both predict (100) near 22.8\u00b0, slightly below the observed 23.0\u00b0. I attribute that small offset to cell or counting error.\n- **Intensities decide between them:** The (100), (210) and (300) reflections are strong (61, 68 and 23). In a structure factor estimate, these reflections scale roughly with the difference between B-site and A-site scattering.\n  - For Na1/3WO3 the heavy W and nearly empty Na site give F(100) \u2248 78. That makes (100) comparable to (110), as observed.\n  - For SrCoO3, F(100) \u2248 3, so (100) would be about 1%. That contradicts the pattern.\n- **Other candidates:** The double perovskite Ba2NbRhO6 (pseudo-cubic \u2248 4.11 \u00c5) and the CaLa3Fe4O12 perovskite (\u2248 3.93 \u00c5, so a weak (100) about 10% and peaks shifted lower) fit worse. The Cs2Ti(WO4)3 pyrochlore and the remaining candidates would give peaks that are not seen.\n- **Second phase:** No unexplained peaks remain, so there is no evidence for one.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1221403\"], \"confidence\": 0.72, \"note\": \"All peaks index to a primitive pseudo-cubic perovskite (a~3.87 A) with strong (100)/(210)/(300) reflections, consistent with the W-dominated tungsten bronze Na(WO3)3 rather than SrCoO3, whose (100) would be nearly absent.\"}",
